from eyes import *
def E(date_iso,date_display,precision,eye,place,headline,digest,source_kind,confidence,source_note,quote=""):
    return dict(date_iso=date_iso,date_display=date_display,precision=precision,eye=eye,place=place,headline=headline,digest=digest,quote=quote,source_kind=source_kind,confidence=confidence,source_note=source_note)
entries=[
E("0306-07-25","25 July 306","day","constantine","Eboracum (York), Britain","His father's army acclaims him emperor at York",
 "Constantius I died at York, and his soldiers at once proclaimed his son emperor. Constantine had been raised at the eastern court of Diocletian and Galerius and had only just rejoined his father. Galerius, the senior emperor, grudgingly accepted him as a junior Caesar. 25 July became his imperial anniversary, the date from which every later jubilee was counted.",
 "secondhand","verified","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 24–25; Eusebius, Life of Constantine I.21–22. The day is the anniversary celebrated in later years."),
E("0306-10-28","28 October 306","day","maxentius","Rome","Rome's guard proclaims Maxentius, six years to the day before the bridge",
 "Angered by new taxes and plans to disband them, the Praetorian Guard and the people of Rome proclaimed Maxentius, son of the retired emperor Maximian. Lactantius later noted that the battle at the Milvian Bridge fell on the anniversary of this day, when Maxentius was completing his sixth year of rule.",
 "secondhand","approx","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 26 and 44; standard modern dating of the proclamation to 28 October 306."),
E("0310","310 (before the summer)","year","constantine","Massilia (Marseille), southern Gaul","Maximian's revolt in Gaul ends in his death",
 "While Constantine was campaigning on the Rhine, his father-in-law Maximian, the old emperor, seized the treasury at Arles and declared himself emperor again. Constantine marched south and besieged him in Massilia. Maximian was captured and soon died; the court said he hanged himself. The orator of 310 speaks against this background.",
 "secondhand","approx","Pan. Lat. VI(7) 14–20; Lactantius, De mort. pers. 29–30. Exact months uncertain."),
E("0310","310 (summer, on the march back to the Rhine)","year","pan310","A temple of Apollo in Gaul, often identified as Grand (Vosges)","Apollo appears to Constantine at 'the most beautiful temple'",
 "On his way back from Massilia, the orator says, Constantine turned aside to a temple of Apollo, 'the most beautiful in the world'. There he saw his Apollo, with Victory, offering him laurel wreaths, each marked with the numeral XXX, a promise of thirty years of rule. The orator adds that Constantine recognised himself in the god, the young ruler of the world foretold by poets. This is the only vision attributed to Constantine by a source written within months of it, and it is pagan.",
 "secondhand","approx","Pan. Lat. VI(7) 21.3–7 (Nixon and Rodgers 1994). The temple's identification with Grand rests on modern scholarship. Peter Weiss (1993) argued that the experience was a solar halo, later retold in Christian form; others reject the link."),
E("0310-08","c. August 310","month","pan310","Trier, imperial palace","A speech gives Constantine a new grandfather and a new god",
 "Before the emperor and court at Trier, the orator announced what he called a fact unknown to most: Constantine descended from the emperor Claudius II, so his rule was inherited, not granted by other emperors. He passed lightly over Maximian's death, praised the new building at Trier and Autun, and set the vision of Apollo near his close.",
 "firsthand","approx","Pan. Lat. VI(7) 2 and 21–22; Ware 2021 (excerpt): delivered at Trier during the city's anniversary, conventionally August 310."),
E("0311-04-30","30 April 311","day","lactantius","Nicomedia (as preserved by Lactantius)","The chief persecutor, dying, ends persecution in his name",
 "Lactantius preserved the text of the edict in which the gravely ill emperor Galerius allowed Christians to exist again and to rebuild their meeting-places, asking them to pray for him. Lactantius reports that it was posted in Nicomedia on the day before the kalends of May. Galerius died within days. For Lactantius this was the first proof of his theme: persecutors die badly.",
 "official record","approx","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 33–35; Eusebius, Church History VIII.17."),
E("0312","312 (before the campaign; place not given)","year","constantine","On a march, place unstated","A cross of light above the noonday sun",
 "As told to Eusebius many years later, Constantine, preparing for war against Maxentius, decided to seek the help of the God his father had honoured, and prayed for a sign. Around midday he saw above the sun a cross of light with writing joined to it: 'Conquer by this'. He and the whole army that was with him on the march were struck with amazement. The account gives no date or place, and some scholars connect it with the experience in Gaul reported in 310.",
 "secondhand","approx","Eusebius, Life of Constantine I.27–28 (written 337–339), NPNF trans. Richardson 1890. Earliest form of this version: c. 337; told to Eusebius after 325.","CONQUER BY THIS"),
E("0312","312 (the night after the sky-sign)","year","constantine","In camp, place unstated","Christ in a dream, and the making of the labarum",
 "According to the Life, Constantine was unsure what the sign meant until he slept, when Christ appeared with the same sign and told him to make a likeness of it as protection in battle. At dawn he called goldsmiths and had a standard made: a tall gilded spear with a crossbar, topped by a wreath of gold and gems holding the letters chi and rho. In this version the dream follows the sky-sign, and the result is a standard, not shield marks.",
 "secondhand","approx","Eusebius, Life of Constantine I.29–31 (after 337)."),
E("0312","Spring–summer 312","range","pan313","Susa, Turin, Verona, northern Italy","He marches on Italy against the soothsayers' advice",
 "The orator of 313 says Constantine crossed the Alps with perhaps a quarter of his army, against the counsel of his officers and the warnings of the haruspices, and took Susa, defeated Maxentius's heavy cavalry near Turin, and besieged Verona. He asks what god, what present power, urged him on, and answers that the divine mind spoke to Constantine alone.",
 "secondhand","approx","Pan. Lat. XII(9) 2–4, 5–13 (Nixon and Rodgers 1994). Dates of Susa, Turin and Verona are inferred."),
E("0312-10-27","Night of 27–28 October 312 (on the eve of battle)","day","lactantius","Constantine's camp north of Rome","The dream command: mark the heavenly sign on the shields",
 "Writing two or three years later, Lactantius says Constantine was warned in his sleep to put the heavenly sign of God on his soldiers' shields and then join battle. He did as commanded, marking them with a letter X turned, its top bent round: a monogram of Christ. Lactantius mentions no sign in the sky and no standard. This is the earliest surviving Christian account.",
 "secondhand","approx","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 44.5 (ANF trans. Fletcher 1886). 'On the eve' is implied by the narrative; the night is not dated in the text. Written c. 314–315.","the heavenly sign to be delineated on the shields of his soldiers"),
E("0312-10-28","28 October 312","day","maxentius","Rome","Maxentius consults the Sibylline books",
 "Lactantius says that while Maxentius stayed in the city, a riot broke out at the games, with the crowd shouting that Constantine could not be beaten. Maxentius ordered the Sibylline books inspected, and they replied that on that day the enemy of the Romans would perish. Taking this as a promise of victory, he left the city to join his army.",
 "secondhand","verified","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 44.7–9 (ANF trans. Fletcher 1886), BKV Fribourg.","On the same day the enemy of the Romans should perish"),
E("0312-10-28","28 October 312","day","constantine","Milvian Bridge and Saxa Rubra, north of Rome","Battle at the bridge; Maxentius drowns in the Tiber",
 "Maxentius's army crossed the Tiber to meet Constantine on the Via Flaminia, with the river at its back. Constantine's cavalry broke them, and in the retreat the temporary bridge of boats gave way; Maxentius fell into the river and drowned, with many of his men. His body was recovered the next day. The victory made Constantine master of the western empire.",
 "secondhand","verified","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 44; Pan. Lat. XII(9) 16–18; Eusebius, Church History IX.9; Zosimus 2.16. The date follows Lactantius (sixth day before the kalends of November)."),
E("0312-10-28","28 October 312 (written c. 498–518)","day","zosimus","Constantinople, writing about Rome","Owls on the wall, and a Sibyl misread",
 "Zosimus says Maxentius sacrificed and consulted the Sibylline oracles, which warned that whoever harmed the Romans would die miserably; he took it as a threat to his attacker. As his army crossed the bridge, owls in great numbers settled on the walls, and Constantine, seeing it, ordered his men forward. The beams of the bridge broke and Maxentius was swept away. There is no Christian sign in this pagan account.",
 "legend","verified","Zosimus, New History 2.16 (London, 1814 translation), Livius.org. Written nearly two centuries after the battle.","an infinite number of owls flew down and covered the wall"),
E("0312-10-29","29 October 312","day","constantine","Rome","The victor enters Rome",
 "The day after the battle Constantine entered Rome in procession. The orator of 313 describes crowds pressing round him and says the head of Maxentius was carried on a spear. He disbanded the Praetorian Guard that had made Maxentius emperor. Later writers say he did not go up to the Capitol to sacrifice to Jupiter, a point still argued.",
 "secondhand","approx","Pan. Lat. XII(9) 18–19; Nazarius, Pan. Lat. IV(10) 31–32. Date from the Roman calendar's commemoration of his entry."),
E("0312-11","November 312","month","senate","Rome, Senate house","The Senate votes him 'Maximus', the senior emperor",
 "Lactantius says the Senate, in recognition of his merits, granted Constantine the title of first and greatest emperor, Maximus, which Maximinus Daia in the East had claimed for himself. Daia was furious when he heard. The Senate also dedicated to Constantine the great basilica Maxentius had built near the Forum.",
 "secondhand","approx","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 44.11–12; Aurelius Victor 40.26 for the basilica."),
E("0312-11","Late 312 or 313","range","eusebius","Rome (statue), described from Caesarea","A statue in Rome holding the saving sign",
 "Eusebius writes that Constantine ordered a statue of himself set up in a public place in Rome, holding in its right hand a tall cross-shaped standard, with a Latin inscription saying that by this saving sign he had freed the city from the tyrant and restored the Senate and people. Eusebius is the only source for this statue and its text; he was far away in Palestine.",
 "secondhand","approx","Eusebius, Church History IX.9.10–11 (edition of c. 313–316); repeated in Life of Constantine I.40."),
E("0313-02","February 313","month","licinius","Milan","Two emperors meet at Milan; a marriage seals the alliance",
 "Licinius came to Milan and married Constantine's half-sister Constantia. The two Augusti agreed on a common policy toward the Christians and the restoration of their property. Nothing was published at Milan itself: the 'Edict of Milan' is the name later given to the letter Licinius sent out in the East after this meeting.",
 "secondhand","approx","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 45 and 48; month from the modern chronology."),
E("0313-04-30","30 April 313 (or 1 May)","day","licinius","Campus Serenus, near Adrianople, Thrace","An angel gives Licinius a prayer before battle",
 "Lactantius says that as Maximinus Daia advanced, an angel stood by the sleeping Licinius and told him to rise and pray with his whole army to the supreme God, promising victory. Licinius had the prayer written out and distributed. On the battlefield his soldiers laid down their shields, took off their helmets, raised their hands and repeated it three times after the emperor. Daia's larger army was routed. The prayer names no Christ and no particular god.",
 "secondhand","approx","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 46–47 (ANF trans. Fletcher 1886), CCEL. Licinius planned to fight on the kalends of May and Daia on the day before; modern accounts usually give 30 April.","Supreme God, we beseech Thee; Holy God, we beseech Thee"),
E("0313-06-13","13 June 313","day","licinius","Nicomedia","The toleration letter is posted where persecution began",
 "Licinius had his letter to the governors posted in Nicomedia. It opens with the meeting of the two emperors at Milan and grants Christians and all others the freedom to follow whatever religion each preferred, so that whatever divinity dwells in heaven might be favourable to the emperors and their subjects. Churches, cemeteries and property taken in the persecution were to be returned without payment.",
 "official record","verified","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 48 (posted on the ides of June, Constantine and Licinius consuls for the third time), BKV Fribourg; Greek version in Eusebius, Church History X.5.","liberty to follow that mode of religion"),
E("0313-06-13","13 June 313","day","lactantius","Nicomedia","A survivor of the persecution copies the letter",
 "Lactantius, who had been in Nicomedia when Diocletian's persecution opened in 303, gave the Latin text of Licinius's letter in full. He presents it as the restoration of the church after ten years and four months of persecution. The letter never names Christ and uses careful, neutral words for God; Lactantius does not comment on that.",
 "official record","verified","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 48, BKV Fribourg."),
E("0313-08","Summer 313","range","lactantius","Tarsus, Cilicia (as reported)","The last persecutor dies at Tarsus",
 "Lactantius describes the death of Maximinus Daia at Tarsus after his defeat by Licinius, presenting it as divine punishment and recording that Daia, in his final illness, confessed the Christian God and begged for mercy. He also reports Licinius's killing of the families of earlier emperors. These pages complete his argument that the gods of the persecutors had failed them.",
 "secondhand","approx","Lactantius, De mort. pers. 49–51. Month approximate."),
E("0313","313 (probably late summer or autumn)","year","pan313","Trier","A prayer to the supreme creator of many names",
 "Back in Gaul, the orator closes his speech by addressing the supreme creator of things, whose names are as many as the languages of men, whether a power spread through the world or one above the heavens. He calls Constantine's victory the work of a divine mind that communicated with him alone, leaving the lesser gods to others. There is no cross, chi-rho or shield sign in the speech.",
 "firsthand","approx","Pan. Lat. XII(9) 2.4–5 and 26 (Nixon and Rodgers 1994). Date within 313 debated."),
E("0314","c. 314–315","range","lactantius","Gaul or Nicomedia (debated)","Lactantius finishes 'On the Deaths of the Persecutors'",
 "Lactantius dedicated the work to a confessor named Donatus who had been imprisoned for six years. It is the source closest in time to the battle that tells the story of a Christian sign, written within about three years of it. It is a polemic, built to show God's judgement on persecutors, and its dream story is short and practical: a command, a sign, a victory.",
 "later firsthand","approx","De mort. pers. 1 and 52; date c. 314–315 rests on internal evidence (it knows Licinius as a friend of the Christians and nothing of the war of 316)."),
E("0315-07","July 315 (decennalia, 25 July)","month","senate","Rome, beside the Colosseum","The arch says: 'by the prompting of the divinity'",
 "For Constantine's tenth anniversary the Senate and People of Rome dedicated a triple arch. Its inscription says he avenged the state on the tyrant and his whole faction with just arms, 'instinctu divinitatis, mentis magnitudine' (by the prompting of the divinity and by the greatness of his mind). The god is not named. A frieze shows the march and the battle at the bridge, with the Sun god among the roundels and Victories in the spandrels, but no Christian sign.",
 "official record","verified","Arch inscription, CIL VI 1139 (text in World History Encyclopedia). Dedication usually placed at the decennalia of 25 July 315."),
E("0315","c. 315","year","ticinum","Ticinum mint","The chi-rho on the emperor's helmet",
 "A silver presentation medallion struck at Ticinum shows Constantine facing forward in a high crested helmet. A small badge with the chi-rho monogram sits on the helmet bowl; he holds a horse's bridle and a shield showing the wolf and twins. The medallion was probably given to officers at the decennalia. It is often called the earliest official image that carries the Christian monogram, though the badge is tiny.",
 "official record","approx","Ticinum silver medallion, c. 315, Staatliche Münzsammlung Munich (bavarikon record); dating by numismatic convention (RIC VII Ticinum 36)."),
E("0318","c. 310–c. 320","range","ticinum","Ticinum and other western mints","The Unconquered Sun stays on the coins",
 "For years after 312 the mints of Ticinum, Rome, Trier and London went on striking coins with the reverse SOLI INVICTO COMITI, 'to the Unconquered Sun, companion', often showing Sol raising his hand. Constantine's public image thus kept the god of the 310 speech alongside the new monogram. The Sun types fade out around 320, before the war with Licinius.",
 "official record","approx","Roman Imperial Coinage VI–VII; end date of Sol types debated (c. 318–325)."),
E("0321-03","Probably March 321","month","nazarius","Rome","Heavenly armies led by the deified Constantius",
 "Nazarius tells his audience that all Gaul talks of armies seen in the sky in 312, shining, saying they had come to help Constantine, led by his father Constantius, now among the gods. He presents this as a marvel already widely told. In 321, nine years after the battle, the official speech still describes the divine help without any Christian sign.",
 "secondhand","approx","Nazarius, Pan. Lat. IV(10) 14 (Nixon and Rodgers 1994); occasion: fifteenth year of Constantine and fifth of the Caesars, 321."),
E("0321-03-07","7 March 321","day","constantine","Imperial chancery (law sent to Helpidius)","The 'venerable day of the Sun' made a day of rest",
 "Constantine ordered that on the day of the Sun judges, city people and craftsmen should rest, though farmers might work their fields if weather required. The law uses the traditional name of the day and no Christian term. Christians already met on that day, as the Lord's Day; the law served both readings.",
 "official record","approx","Codex Justinianus 3.12.2; date as transmitted in the Code."),
E("0324-09-18","18 September 324","day","licinius","Chrysopolis, opposite Byzantium","Licinius is defeated; one emperor rules again",
 "After a first war in 316 and years of tension, Constantine defeated Licinius at Adrianople and then at Chrysopolis. Licinius surrendered on the plea of his wife Constantia and was sent to Thessalonica, where he was put to death in 325. Eusebius, who had praised him in earlier editions of the Church History, now recast him as a persecutor.",
 "secondhand","approx","Eusebius, Life of Constantine II.1–18; Church History X.8–9 (revised edition); Zosimus 2.22–28. Date of Chrysopolis as usually given."),
E("0325-06","May–June 325","range","eusebius","Nicaea, Bithynia","At Nicaea, the historian meets the emperor",
 "Eusebius attended the council that Constantine summoned to Nicaea and later described the emperor entering the hall in robes that flashed like light. The council agreed a creed and a rule for keeping Easter. Some scholars think it was around this time, or at his later visits to court, that Eusebius heard Constantine tell the story of the sign.",
 "later firsthand","approx","Eusebius, Life of Constantine III.6–14 (written after 337); the time of the conversation about the vision is not stated in the text."),
E("0326","326","year","constantine","Pola, Istria, and Rome","The execution of Crispus",
 "In 326 Constantine had his eldest son Crispus, Lactantius's former pupil and the victor of the naval war against Licinius, put to death at Pola; his wife Fausta died soon after. No contemporary explains why. Eusebius's Life passes over both deaths in silence.",
 "secondhand","approx","Ammianus Marcellinus 14.11.20; Zosimus 2.29; Eutropius 10.6. Causes unknown."),
E("0326","326 (story written c. 498–518)","year","zosimus","Constantinople, writing about Rome","A hostile story of why Constantine turned Christian",
 "Zosimus says that after killing Crispus and Fausta, Constantine sought purification; pagan priests refused, and an Egyptian from Spain told him that the Christian faith could wash away any sin. Only then, in this version, did he abandon the ancestral rites. The story contradicts every earlier account, which put his turn in 312, and Christian writers rejected it.",
 "legend","approx","Zosimus, New History 2.29, Livius.org (book 2)."),
E("0330-05-11","11 May 330","day","constantine","Byzantium, renamed Constantinople","The new city is dedicated",
 "Constantine dedicated the refounded city of Byzantium as his capital, with a column carrying his statue in the new forum. Later sources describe both Christian churches and the old civic cults in the new city, and disagree on how far it was meant to be Christian. The city kept his name until the Ottoman conquest of 1453 and after.",
 "official record","approx","Chronicon Paschale for the date; Eusebius, Life of Constantine III.48."),
E("0336-07-25","25 July 336","day","eusebius","Constantinople, imperial palace","A speech for the thirty years the vision promised",
 "Eusebius spoke at court for Constantine's thirtieth year of rule (tricennalia). He portrayed the emperor as the image of the divine Word on earth, ruling under the saving sign. Constantine had now reached the thirty years that the orator of 310 said Apollo's wreaths foretold; Eusebius, of course, gives the credit to Christ.",
 "firsthand","approx","Eusebius, In Praise of Constantine (Tricennial Oration); delivery usually placed on 25 July 336."),
E("0337-05-22","22 May 337 (Pentecost)","day","constantine","Achyron, near Nicomedia","Baptised on his deathbed, Constantine dies",
 "Falling ill after Easter, Constantine went to the baths near Helenopolis, then to a villa outside Nicomedia, where bishops (led by Eusebius of Nicomedia) baptised him. He put off the purple and died in white robes at noon on Pentecost. His body was taken to Constantinople and buried in the Church of the Holy Apostles.",
 "secondhand","approx","Eusebius, Life of Constantine IV.61–71."),
E("0337-06","337–339","range","eusebius","Caesarea","Writing the Life: 'he confirmed it by an oath'",
 "In the Life, Eusebius introduces the vision with care. If anyone else had told it, he writes, it would not be easy to believe; but the emperor himself told it long afterwards, when Eusebius was honoured with his acquaintance, and swore to it. Eusebius adds that the army saw it too. It is the latest of the three main accounts, the only one claiming to come from Constantine, and the only one with a sign in the sky.",
 "secondhand","verified","Eusebius, Life of Constantine I.28 (NPNF trans. Richardson 1890), CCEL and Fordham Sourcebook. Gap: about 25 years between the event and the written account.","confirmed his statement by an oath"),
E("0337-06","337–339","range","eusebius","Caesarea","'I have seen it': the labarum described",
 "Eusebius describes the jewelled standard made after the dream in detail and says he himself had seen it, with a banner and portraits of the emperor and his sons beneath the wreath. He adds that Constantine used copies of it as a protection at the head of all his armies. The standard he saw was a later object; whether it matched the first one is unknown.",
 "later firsthand","approx","Eusebius, Life of Constantine I.30–31 and II.7–9."),
E("0339-05","c. 339","year","eusebius","Caesarea","Eusebius dies with the Life unfinished",
 "Eusebius died about two years after Constantine, leaving the Life without its final revision; scholars see unfinished joins in it. His two accounts, the visionless one of the Church History and the cross of light of the Life, were both passed down and copied side by side.",
 "secondhand","approx","Date of death traditional (c. 339); Cameron and Hall's commentary on the Life."),
]
